  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a programmable photonic or metasurface platform can perform several optical computing functions by modulating phase pixels. Overall, it presents the same hardware as a flexible processor that can route different wavelengths to different tasks, generate multiple outputs from one input, and implement neural-network-style matrix operations.\n\nAt the top left, different inputs at separate wavelengths are sent through a shared modulated pixel array, with each wavelength assigned to a distinct function such as digit classification, imaging, or logic operations. At the top right, a single input is processed by AC-modulated phase pixels to produce different harmonic outputs, suggesting multitasking in which separate frequency components encode different labels, such as animal type, colour, or breed.\n\nThe middle row compares two architectures for matrix–vector multiplication: a photonic front end followed by a digital neural-network back end, versus a more integrated co-designed system where the programmable photonic matrix operation sits between digital input and output stages.\n\nThe bottom schematic shows a deeper programmable metasurface neural network. An input digit passes through multiple modulated layers, with amplitude and phase patterns evolving through the network. Structural nonlinearity links the layers, and the final optical pattern corresponds to a classification output, highlighting all-optical inference.",
